  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThe dry blocky pattern remains in place through the end of the week \nwith temperatures continuing to warm a degree or two each day.  The \nupper ridge shifts east late in the week into the weekend which \nbrings the western long wave trough across the Rockies into the \nGreat Plains Saturday/Saturday night.  The GFS pushes a cold front \ninto the Midwest on Sunday, and generates some light rain across \nnorthern Missouri into central Illinois.  The EC is slower with this \nprogression of the upper pattern and therefore with the cold front \nas well.  The LREF mean appears to favor the slower and warmer \nsolution, with temperatures not really beginning to fall until \nMonday.  However the cluster analysis shows a great deal of \nuncertainty in both the speed and amplitude of the trough moving \nthrough the Midwest.  Max T IQRs widen from 2-4 degrees in most \nlocations through Sunday, to as much as 15 degrees on Monday.  The \nNBM does cool temperatures off a few degrees on Monday, although it \ndoes not introduce any PoPs on this run.  Think this is a reasonable \nstart to what should be an inevitable cool down, although confidence \nis low in the ultimate timing for this update.",
